 ## Overview
-Splatting is an object-order volume rendering technique where volumetric data is decomposed into primitives (typically Gaussians or disks) that are projected onto the image plane as overlapping footprints and accumulated via alpha blending [S2, S4, unofficial][S7, unofficial]. It solves the problem of efficiently rendering dense volumetric data by leveraging hardware-accelerated rasterization and cache-coherent traversal, contrasting with image-order ray-casting which samples the volume per-pixel [S2, unofficial][S14, unofficial]. Unlike [Shear-warp](24-shear-warp.md), which relies on affine warping of slices, splatting handles perspective distortion through per-primitive footprint calculation (e.g., EWA) or sheet-buffer compositing [S4, unofficial][S11, abstract only, unofficial].
+Splatting is an object-order volume rendering technique where volumetric data is decomposed into primitives (typically Gaussians or disks) that are projected onto the image plane as overlapping footprints and accumulated via alpha blending [S2, unofficial][S4, unofficial][S7, unofficial]. It solves the problem of efficiently rendering dense volumetric data by leveraging hardware-accelerated rasterization and cache-coherent traversal, contrasting with image-order ray-casting which samples the volume per-pixel [S2, unofficial][S14, unofficial]. Unlike [Shear-warp](24-shear-warp.md), which relies on affine warping of slices, splatting handles perspective distortion through per-primitive footprint calculation (e.g., EWA) or sheet-buffer compositing [S4, unofficial][S11, abstract only, unofficial].
 
 ## Shared principles
-The splatting family shares the fundamental mechanism of treating volumetric data as a set of geometric or parametric primitives that are transformed and rasterized [G].
+The splatting family shares the fundamental mechanism of treating volumetric data as a set of geometric or parametric primitives that are transformed and rasterized [S1, unofficial][S3, unofficial][S4, unofficial].
 
 1.  **Primitive Representation:** The volume is decomposed into primitives [G]. Each primitive $k$ is defined by its position $\mathbf{p}_k$, density/extinction coefficient $g_k$ or $\rho_k$, and a reconstruction kernel $h$ (typically a Gaussian) [S1, unofficial][S6, unofficial][S10, unofficial].
-2.  **Transformation:** Primitives are transformed from volume space to screen space using the Model-View-Projection (MVP) matrix or specific factorizations (e.g., shear-warp) [S2, S3, S4, unofficial]. For perspective projections, the kernel shape may be distorted (e.g., EWA splatting) to account for foreshortening [S6, unofficial][S10, unofficial].
