+### AMR Splatting with FFT
+A volume rendering technique for AMR data where cells are converted into points with size information, projected to 2D, and convolved with a Gaussian kernel using FFT to produce a summation plot [S22, unofficial]. It converts AMR cells into points with additional size information depending on the AMR cell size [S22, unofficial]. It computes a different map for every AMR level involved in the camera-defined image [S22, unofficial]. For every level, it projects corresponding AMR cell center points onto the projection plane [S22, unofficial]. It transforms the 1D projected points into 2D disks by doing a convolution product with a 2D Gaussian kernel [S22, unofficial]. It performs the convolution using the Fast Fourier Transform (FFT), as multiplication in Fourier space corresponds to a convolution product [S22, unofficial]. It adds all maps obtained for every level to produce the final image [S22, unofficial]. Implementation platform is CPU using Numpy's FFT module [S22, unofficial]. Grid alignment may appear on resulting images [S22, unofficial]. Solutions include increasing splatted disk/point sizes or adding a random shift to point position, though these lead to lower image quality [S22, unofficial]. It produces images close to ray-traced Maximum Intensity Projection (MIP) maps [S22, unofficial].
 
