-#### AMR Splatting with FFT
-A volume rendering technique for AMR data where cells are converted into points with size information, projected to 2D, and convolved with a Gaussian kernel using FFT to produce a summation plot [S25]. It converts AMR cells into points with additional size information depending on the AMR cell size. It computes a different map for every AMR level involved in the camera-defined image. For every level, it projects corresponding AMR cell center points onto the projection plane. It transforms the 1D projected points into 2D disks by doing a convolution product with a 2D Gaussian kernel. It performs the convolution using the Fast Fourier Transform (FFT), as multiplication in Fourier space corresponds to a convolution product. It adds all maps obtained for every level to produce the final image [S25]. Implementation platform is CPU using Numpy's FFT module [S25]. Grid alignment may appear on resulting images. Solutions include increasing splatted disk/point sizes or adding a random shift to point position, though these lead to lower image quality [S25]. It produces images close to ray-traced Maximum Intensity Projection (MIP) maps [S25].
+### REdiSplats
+A method that ray traces flat 3D Gaussians aligned to mesh-based disks, allowing control of Gaussians by editing mesh vertices [S25].
 
