+### Point-Based Neural Renderer Architecture
+A neural network that encodes rasterized point clouds from multiple input views, pools features with inductive bias for visibility and temporal smoothing, and decodes them to generate the final rendering [S28]. It generates attribute layers for each view: input-view colors and optimized latent features reprojected to the novel view using point splatting [S28]. It encodes each view in a feature space of F dimensions using convolutional residual blocks with shared weights across all input views [S28]. It applies weighted average pooling to produce a single F-channel image using the final weights w [S28]. It decodes the pooled features using consecutive convolutional residual blocks to generate the final rendering [S28]. Feature space dimension F is typically 64 [S28]. Unlike previous neural renderers, this architecture allows changing the number of selected views without retraining because weighted average pooling normalizes the sum of weights per pixel [S28].
+
+### Probabilistic Depth Testing for Point-Based Rendering
+A method to determine visibility in novel views by incorporating depth uncertainty from MVS reconstruction, replacing hard visibility tests with soft probabilistic weights to prevent discarding valuable information from noisy data [S28].
+
+### Hit Pixel Count Renderer
+Project Gaussians: call GSPlatRenderer.project with means3D, scales, rotations, viewpoint_camera, scaling_modifier, block_size, extra_projection_kwargs to get xys, depths, radii, conics, comp, num_tiles_hit, cov3d [S29, unofficial]. Anti-aliasing: if anti_aliased is True, multiply opacities by comp[:, None] [S29, unofficial]. Compute hit pixel count: call hit_pixel_count with xys, depths, radii, conics, num_tiles_hit, opacities, img_height, img_width, block_width; return count, opacity_score, alpha_score, visibility_score [S29, unofficial]. scaling_modifier is float = 1.0 [S29, unofficial]. anti_aliased is bool = True [S29, unofficial]. block_size is int = 16 [S29, unofficial].
+
+### A-buffer Blending for Splatting
+A blending technique using an A-buffer to remove high-frequency noise at patch boundaries and simulate a painterly effect while preserving individual splat curvature [S30]. For all pixels $pix_{uv}$: If all fragments are sorted, retrieve the result from the A-buffer. If not, perform a step of bubble sort on the sorted fragments, perform an alpha blend step, update the A-buffer, and set the result [S30]. Two-pass weighted Gaussian blur is implemented as an alternative for moving objects or camera navigation, where A-buffering might be too expensive [S30].
 
 ## Background
-Splatting was first proposed by Westover [S4, unofficial]. It is an object-order approach, contrasting with ray-casting which is image-order [S4, unofficial][S14, unofficial]. The term is derived from the analogy of throwing snowballs (volume elements) at a wall (image plane), where each snowball leaves a fuzzy circular mark (footprint) [S6, unofficial]. Splatting is considered one of the four main paradigms of volume rendering [S9, book]. It is particularly effective for generating X-ray projection images from volumes, as the summation of pre-integrated reconstruction kernels is faster and more accurate than ray-casting for this specific output [S2, unofficial]. The method is also used in 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS), where millions of 3D Gaussians are optimized by tweaking parameters until the rendered image matches a real image [S5, S13, unofficial].
